roduction you need this suitably  large underground network of pipelines  to move  those products to export terminals to  power plants  to refineries and petrochemical  facilities these are not  small ventures they're thousands of  miles long just for one pipeline alone  they cost billions of dollars they  require massive  permitting at the federal level  and so it was that the shale revolution  led to a pipeline boom  but that boom ultimately triggered an  explosion of lawsuits broadly led by  environmentalists  no project embodies the herculean nature  and divisiveness of this business  better than the keystone xl pipeline  the keystone xl pipeline is like a  diagonal line  from the middle of alberta down to  houston texas  it cuts through the u.s states of  montana south dakota  nebraska kansas oklahoma and  texas a project of this scale  faces varying permitting systems from  state to state  as well as multiple run-ins with a host  of conflicting interest groups  the keystone xl pipeline embodied a lot  of the elements that pipeline opponents  hated so much about the industry it was  massive you know more than a thousand  miles long  it carried oil from the alberta oil  sands which a lot of  folks call tar sands because it's a  particularly heavy grade of crude  it went through private land indigenous  land  it wasn't even carrying american oil and  gas so i think  a lot of folks saw it as something that  represented everything they hated  about the fossil fuel and extraction  industry  this is environmental activist anthony  swift from the natural resources defense  council  and suffice it to say he's not a fan of  oil pipelines  keystone xl was a 830 000  barrel a day uh tar sands pipeline that  would have expanded  the dirtiest source of oil in the world  for decades to come  a scenario that would have been  inconsistent with a safe climate and in  addition to that  it would have had devastating impacts to  indigenous communities in alberta  and communities a