line  project  the state department has decided that  the keystone  xl pipeline would not serve the national  interests of the united states  let me just say that life has a way of  surprising us  what a great honor to be able to  introduce for the first time  ever anywhere the 45th president of the  united states of america donald j  trump  [Applause]  trump giving them a permit in early 2017  keystone pipeline legal interventions  caused that permit to have to be  reissued  back in 2019 and it took that length of  time before trans-canada and its  shippers  who had to be reassembled commercially  started to construct the project  in 2020 the pendulum once again  swung in the other direction when  shortly after his inauguration  president biden halted the pipeline's  phase 4 construction which was to cut  over 500 miles  and run straight through a national  landmark area in nebraska  called the sandhills i'm visiting you  from  oyen alberta and until a few days ago  this was kind of ground  zero for construction of the keystone xl  pipeline  that all pretty much came to a halt a  few days ago  this was something that was enormously  controversial it had this kind of  outsized  impact on the way we talk about our  energy and  climate in the us it was something that  he promised to do and he did it  i think it was seen as kind of an easy  way  for biden to quickly deliver on some of  his environmental promises because  at the end of the day this was a  pipeline that was not yet built  it is a lot harder to go after something  that has 200  miles of steel already in the ground  biden's cancellation of the keystone xl  project  was you know an absolutely necessary  decision  it upheld the decision that president  obama made in 2016  after a thorough review and it just  shows that he's serious about  transitioning the us to clean energy  and really taking a leadership stance  on climate change globally but focusing  their energy on the pipelines  is often seen by people in the industry  as misdirected activism  this doesn't stop oil sands production  it doesn't stop canadians trying to find  other ways of getting that oil into the  united states  but it does reduce the economic value of  having  lost the most efficient option that you  could have resorted  when we think about the  impact that the keystone xl pipeline  would have had on the oil market i think  is when  we start to see even opinions in the  energy industry divided  there are signs that there is the  shortage of so-called takeaway capacity  out of alberta  however on the other side it's not clear  that we needed  you know a pipeline that could shuttle  800 000 barrels a day  plus out of canada so i think  the impact and the fallout from this  decision is going to remain to be seen  a major point that pro pipeline  advocates emphasize is of course jobs  and while it's true that killing a  pipeline does result in a loss of some  work  that might be a rather myopic definition  of jobs  as far as jobs the industry has said  that it would have employed over 10  000 direct jobs obviously that would  have a pretty big impact on certain  communities  and in certain circles where there are a  lot of industrial workers  i think it's important to remember that  these were not permanent positions  these were mostly construction jobs and  pipeline development jobs on the ground  it's a little bit  of a more complicated picture than i  think the industry  has made it sound there really are  several different economic interests who  were impacted by this  so there are the people like transcanada  energy  and their other partners in the pipeline  notably the alberta government  province of alberta you know they stand  to lose somewhere my guess would be on  the order of four billion dollars  all of that is kind of like the sunk  cost of just trying to do the project  states that lose some of the economic  benefits that derive from the project  such as property tax payments that  lit