egal development  that arises  that's adding cost to your project and  that is the absolute opposite of what  these investors want to see  it's just been dramatic how quickly  these new projects had to basically stop  in the face of all of this  environmental opposition you'd see  companies like williams who  tried to build not one but two gas  pipelines in  the northeast and got shut down by the  state of new york both times  energy transfer with the dakota access  pipeline the rover gas pipeline  both of those encountered tremendous  opposition from environmental groups but  even from  regulators themselves even in texas  which we never would have expected to  see very much opposition here kinder  morgan had trouble  getting a natural gas pipeline built  recently so i think it's just becoming  clear  that this is not an industry that's  going to be able to just grow and grow  and grow like it used to be  there's still people who who think that  keystone exile will  will be revived maybe with a new  administration  it's a bit uncertain how much need there  is for the pipeline  will there be even the impetus to drive  ahead  it's a pretty clear win for the  environmental movement this is something  that they have  sought to kill for over a decade now  the american environmental movement won  because  it attached itself to the democratic  candidate  that person one he followed through on  his commitment that he would deny this  project  biden's policy goals are set by  an international community that is  aligned at maintaining a safe  climate and that means that projects  like keystone xl are simply inconsistent  with that goal  the environmental community is aligned  with the biden administration's  goal of maintaining a safe climate and  that was what  the president was elected to achieve by  the american people  when we look at the pipeline industry as  a whole i think it's a little bit more  nuanced  for some companies keystone xl being  dead  actually might save